  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/a7a711eed2b4805f0e9dfc64c0992acfef26430e37f8df446b08055143a6cadc.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,5)$ and lies on $y=f.$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(5,2)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=4,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(5)?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $B\\,(5,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(5)=2.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{\u2032}(2)=4.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{\u2032}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{\u2032}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
